## Proof

Let $z = x + iy$ with $y = \operatorname{Im}(z) > 0$. Then
$$e^{2\pi i n z} = e^{2\pi i n x}\, e^{-2\pi n y},$$
so the $n$-th term of the series is
$$a_n\, e^{2\pi i n z} = a_n\, e^{2\pi i n x}\, e^{-2\pi n y},$$
with absolute value $|a_n|\, e^{-2\pi n y}$.

**Step 1: Bounding the coefficients.** Since $f$ is bounded on the upper half-plane, there exists $M > 0$ with $|f(z)| \le M$ for all $\operatorname{Im}(z) > 0$. For fixed $y > 0$, the function $x \mapsto f(x+iy)$ is a bounded $1$-periodic function of $x$, and its Fourier coefficients are exactly $a_n\, e^{-2\pi n y}$:
$$a_n\, e^{-2\pi n y} = \int_0^1 f(x+iy)\, e^{-2\pi i n x}\, dx.$$
Therefore
$$|a_n|\, e^{-2\pi n y} \le \int_0^1 |f(x+iy)|\, dx \le M.$$
This holds for **every** $y > 0$. Letting $y \to 0^+$ gives
$$|a_n| \le M \qquad \text{for all } n \ge 1.$$

**Step 2: Decay of individual terms.** The $n$-th term satisfies
$$\bigl|a_n\, e^{2\pi i n z}\bigr| = |a_n|\, e^{-2\pi n y} \le M\, e^{-2\pi n y}.$$
Thus each term $a_n\, e^{2\pi i n z}$ **decays exponentially** in $y = \operatorname{Im}(z)$ at rate $e^{-2\pi n y}$; in particular, the $n$-th term is $O\!\bigl(e^{-2\pi n\, \operatorname{Im}(z)}\bigr)$ as $\operatorname{Im}(z) \to \infty$.

**Step 3: Decay of the full series.** Using $|a_n| \le M$,
$$|f(z)| \le \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} |a_n|\, e^{-2\pi n y} \le M \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} e^{-2\pi n y} = M\,\frac{e^{-2\pi y}}{1 - e^{-2\pi y}}.$$
As $y \to \infty$, the denominator $1 - e^{-2\pi y} \to 1$, so
$$f(z) = O\!\bigl(e^{-2\pi\, \operatorname{Im}(z)}\bigr) \qquad \text{as } \operatorname{Im}(z) \to \infty.$$

**Conclusion.** The boundedness of $f$ on the upper half-plane forces the Fourier coefficients to be uniformly bounded ($|a_n| \le M$). Consequently, the $n$-th term $a_n\, e^{2\pi i n z}$ decays exponentially at rate $e^{-2\pi n\, \operatorname{Im}(z)}$, and the series as a whole decays at least as fast as $O\!\bigl(e^{-2\pi\, \operatorname{Im}(z)}\bigr)$ as $\operatorname{Im}(z) \to \infty$.

